---
layout: article
title: Attack mode
description: Turn on Attack mode in Appwrite Firewall to challenge every visitor to a site until you turn it off.
---

**Attack mode** is a one-click [challenge](/docs/products/firewall/actions#challenge) for a [site](/docs/products/sites) you are viewing in Firewall. Use it when you need to put a proof-of-work challenge in front of all visitors quickly, for example during a traffic spike or abuse incident.

Attack mode is available only for **Sites**. It does not apply to the project API or to Functions. Select a site in the Firewall resource picker to see the **Attack mode** button.

# Turn it on {% #turn-it-on %}

1. Open **Firewall** in your project.
2. Select the site you want to protect.
3. Click **Attack mode**.
4. Confirm **Turn on**.

While Attack mode is on, an amber banner appears under the Firewall header: **Attack mode is on. Every visitor is challenged.** The banner is scoped to the site you have selected. Switching to the API or another site hides it, even if Attack mode is still on for a different site.

# What it creates {% #what-it-creates %}

Appwrite creates (or re-enables) a **Challenge** rule named **Attack mode** on that site. The rule:

- Uses a catch-all condition: **path starts with `/`** (empty paths are stored as `/`, so every request for the site matches)
- Uses [priority](/docs/products/firewall/priority) `0`, so it is evaluated before the create-wizard default of `100`
- Uses the platform default challenge type, with difficulty `3` and a TTL of `1800` seconds

The rule appears in the site's rules list like any other rule. You can inspect it there, but prefer the **Attack mode** button or banner to turn it off so the same rule can be re-enabled later.

If you edit the rule's difficulty or TTL by hand, turning Attack mode off and on again keeps your values rather than resetting them.

Challenged traffic appears in [traffic overview](/docs/products/firewall/monitor) under the **Challenged** series.

# Allowlists {% #allowlists %}

[Bypass](/docs/products/firewall/actions#bypass) rules with a **lower** priority number still run first and can allowlist trusted traffic. Bypass rules at `10` or higher are evaluated after Attack mode, so they do not skip the challenge while Attack mode is on.

To keep an allowlist during Attack mode, give those bypass rules a negative priority. The Console's priority field starts at `0`, so create or update those rules through the API.

# Turn it off {% #turn-it-off %}

Click **Attack mode** again, or **Turn off** on the warning banner, and confirm. Appwrite **disables** the Attack mode rule. It stays in the project (and still counts toward [plan limits](/docs/products/firewall/rules#plan-limits)) so you can turn it back on without creating a new rule.

Your other Firewall rules stay in place.

# Plan limits {% #plan-limits %}

Turning Attack mode on for the first time **creates** a rule. If the project is already at its Firewall rule limit, create a slot by [deleting a rule](/docs/products/firewall/rules#plan-limits) or [upgrade your plan](/docs/advanced/billing) before you can turn Attack mode on.

